                           Emacs Web Server

DESCRIPTION
    A web server in Emacs running handlers written in Emacs Lisp.

REQUIREMENTS
    Emacs 24.3 or later.

STATUS
    Supports HTTP GET and POST requests including URL-encoded
    parameters, multipart/form data and file uploads.  Supports web
    sockets.  Reasonably performant, faster than Elnode [1].  This is
    a new project without much extended use so there are likely bugs
    and potentially security issues.  That said it consists of little
    more than HTTP header parsing logic perched atop Emacs' existing
    network process primitives, so it should be fairly robust.

    [1]  http://eschulte.github.io/emacs-web-server/benchmark/

USAGE
    See the examples/ directory in this repository for examples
    demonstrating usage.  The Emacs web-server is also used to run a
    paste server [2], serve editable Org-mode pages [3] and serve
    files for Cask [4].

    [2]  https://github.com/eschulte/el-sprunge
    [3]  https://github.com/eschulte/org-ehtml
    [4]  https://github.com/cask/cask

    Available from the GNU ELPA [5].  The tutorials page [6] walks
    through usage scenarios including installing the Emacs web-server
    and running it behind a proxy.

    [5]  http://elpa.gnu.org/
    [6]  http://eschulte.github.io/emacs-web-server/tutorials/

    Run `make check' to run the included test suite.

OWNER-THREAD CALLS
    ws-call-on-owner SERVER FUNCTION accepts a callback for the thread that
    called ws-start.  Its return t means accepted, not FUNCTION's value or a
    delivered HTTP reply.  The owner must service process output.  Threaded
    calls always queue, including calls from the owner.  Without make-thread
    support, detected at start time, callbacks run directly.  Callback errors
    are reported and contained in both paths.

    Each server captures the positive ws-owner-call-queue-limit (default 64).
    A full queue or stopped server signals ws-owner-call-refused, with first
    condition datum queue-full or stopped.  A failed notification refuses
    with wake-failed and retracts the call.  Refused callbacks never execute.
    A callback frees its pending slot before running, outside the mutex.
    Each drain has a finite budget no larger than the captured limit.

    The wake resource is one verified private loopback TCP channel, with two
    owner-locked endpoints.  Its temporary listener closes on first accept;
    the accepted peer must match the sender's local endpoint.  Setup is
    bounded and partial resources are cleaned on failure.  The mutex covers
    admission, one coalesced binary wake byte, and the stop fence.  Workers
    only enqueue and write that notification; they never create, wait for,
    read or close a process.  All channel dispatch and cleanup run on owner.
    Unexpected channel loss visibly stops the server rather than silently
    abandoning accepted calls while reporting a live service.

    ws-stop fences admission and discards queued calls before responder
    ON-CLOSE callbacks and channel cleanup.  No callback starts after stop.
    ws-respond, ws-responder-abort and ws-stop signal ws-owner-thread-error
    before mutation if entered off-owner.  ws-respond retains its synchronous
    written-or-nil contract; use it inside the queued callback for deferred
    replies.  These guards prevent those illegal entries.  They do not fix
    independent Emacs process/thread abort defects or establish affinity for
    application callbacks outside this server.

DOCUMENTATION
    Run `make doc' to build the texinfo documentation, also available
    online [6].

    [6]  http://eschulte.github.io/emacs-web-server
